# ==== T-FMT-C · closure body — suite-layout form preservation
#
# AXIS (6 cells): single-`return` next-line · multi-stmt next-line ·
#   inline expression · destructure-param inline · destructure-param
#   next-line · inline `throw` body.
#
# ⚠ CELL NAMING (Core #12): the collapsing shape pre-fix was specifically the
#   SINGLE-post-prelude-`return` one. A multi-statement next-line body was
#   already preserved, so a fixture named "closure block bodies are preserved"
#   that only carried the multi-statement shape would have been GREEN while
#   the defect ran. Each cell below names the shape it samples.
#
# ⚠ The closure emitter is layout-GATED, not layout-ONLY. On the `Inline`
#   side it still destructures the body to pick the expression form:
#   `(int x): return x * 2` renders as `(int x): x * 2` (pre-existing and
#   deliberately unchanged — the parser wraps an inline closure body in
#   `Block { Return(expr) }`, so `return` is a spelling the parser invented,
#   not one the author spelled), while an inline `throw` body is a synthetic
#   `Block { Throw }` that has no expression form and routes through
#   `try_inline_single_terminal_stmt`.
#
# RED pre-fix (recorded 2026-08-11):
#   * single-`return` next-line   — COLLAPSED to `(int x): x * 2`.
#   * destructure next-line       — COLLAPSED the same way.
#   * multi-stmt next-line        — body kept, but the enclosing var-decl
#                                   gained a spurious BLANK line.
#   * EVERY block-bodied closure  — the header emitted `": "` unconditionally
#                                   and then newlined, leaving `(int x): `
#                                   with TRAILING WHITESPACE (measured with
#                                   `cat -A`). The harness asserts no line in
#                                   the output ends in whitespace, so this
#                                   file pins that cell too.
# GREEN pre-fix / BREAK-AND-WATCH: the multi-stmt next-line cell's LAYOUT
#   (only its blank was red) and the two inline cells. Make the closure
#   emitter ignore `Block.layout` and the next-line cells collapse.

void single_return_next():
    int(int) f = (int x):
        return x * 2
    print(f(3))

void multi_stmt_next():
    int(int) g = (int x):
        int t = x * 2
        return t + 1
    print(g(3))

void inline_expr():
    int(int) h = (int x): x * 2
    print(h(3))

void destructure_inline():
    int((int, int)) p = ((int a, int b)): a + b
    print(p((1, 2)))

void destructure_next():
    int((int, int)) q = ((int a, int b)):
        return a + b
    print(q((1, 2)))

void inline_throw():
    int(int) k = (int x): throw "nope"
    print(1)

void main():
    single_return_next()
    multi_stmt_next()
    inline_expr()
    destructure_inline()
    destructure_next()
    inline_throw()
